y point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(x\\) lies on \\(V_x\\).  \nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n1. **Choice of diagonals to be replaced.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   C\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |C|=k .\n   \\]\n\n   (Such a subset exists because \\(k\\le n\\).)\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) pick the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_c=(1,c-1)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Apply Lemma 2 to obtain a sunny line \\(L_c\\) that contains only \\(P_c\\) from \\(T_n\\).  \n   The lines \\(\\{L_c\\mid c\\in C\\}\\) are distinct because the points \\(P_c\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Vertical lines.**  \n   Take all vertical lines \\(V_x\\) for \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\).  \n   There are exactly \\(n-1\\) of them, and none of them is sunny.\n\n4. **Remaining diagonals.**  \n   For every \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus C\\) keep the diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n   These lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are not sunny.\n\n5. **The final family of lines**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   \\mathcal L=\n   \\bigl\\{L_c\\mid c\\in C\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{V_x\\mid 1\\le x\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{D_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus C\\bigr\\}\n   \\;}\n   \\]\n\n   The three groups are pairwise disjoint, so  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L|=k+(n-1)+(n-k)=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_c\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=n\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le 1\\) and \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\).  \nHence the sum \\(c=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and, because \\(c\\ge n+1\\), we have \\(c\\notin C\\).  \nTherefore the diagonal \\(D_c\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\), which is always included in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThus it is covered regardless of the value of its sum.\n\n*Case 3: The point is one of the specially chosen points \\(P_c\\) (i.e. \\(a=1,\\;b=c-1\\) with \\(c\\in C\\)).*  \nSuch a point also lies on the vertical line \\(V_1\\), but more importantly it lies on the sunny line \\(L_c\\).  \nHence it is certainly covered.\n\nAll possibilities are exhausted, so every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nA set of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, because each line is either sunny or not.  \nTherefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.\n\nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
