            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. their slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (isolated sunny line)\n\nFor any \\(P=(p_x,p_y)\\in T_n\\) there exists a line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) whose slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand such that \\(L\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider only non‑vertical lines; a vertical line has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is therefore not sunny.  \nFor a finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\; y-p_y=\\lambda(x-p_x). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_\\lambda\\) also passes through another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct extra point of \\(T_n\\) determines a single rational value of \\(\\lambda\\).  \nSince \\(T_n\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  \n\nAdd the two forbidden slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) to obtain  \n\n\\[\nS' = S\\cup\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nstill a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).  \nChoose a rational number \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus S'\\); such a \\(\\lambda\\) exists because \\(\\mathbb Q\\) is infinite.  \nThen \\(\\lambda\\) is finite, different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), and (2) cannot hold for any \\((a,b)\\neq P\\).  \nConsequently the line \\(L_\\lambda\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at \\(P\\) and is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (vertical columns)\n\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(2\\le x\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_x:\\;x=\\text{constant}=x .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(x\\) lies on \\(V_x\\).  \nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are **not** sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nWithin \\(T_n\\) this line contains exactly the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(-1\\); therefore it is not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n1. **Base family**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal B=\\bigl\\{V_x\\mid 2\\le x\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{D_{n+1}\\}.\n   \\]\n   \\(|\\mathcal B|=(n-2)+1=n-1\\).  \n   No line of \\(\\mathcal B\\) is sunny.  \n   All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal B\\) except the points of column 1, i.e. the set  \n\n   \\[\n   C_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Choosing sunny lines**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *If \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Add the vertical line \\(V_1\\) to \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \n   The resulting family \\(\\mathcal B\\cup\\{V_1\\}\\) has \\(n\\) lines, none of which is